data.content:
Based on my inspection of the cssdoc JSON models used by renderers/tinymce, here's what I found:

## Summary

**Modifier aliases in the JSON are represented in three distinct ways:**

### 1. **Explicit `alias` Field** (Newer pattern)
Some modifiers have a dedicated `"alias"` object pointing to their canonical replacement:

```json
{
  "name": "-color-error",
  "prop": "color",
  "value": "error",
  "alias": {
    "canonical": "-color-danger"
  }
}
```

### 2. **`deprecated` Field** (For replaced APIs)
Older or replaced modifiers use a `deprecated` field:

```json
{
  "name": "-color-accent1",
  "prop": "color",
  "value": "accent1",
  "deprecated": {
    "canonical": "-color-blue"
  }
}
```

### 3. **Description-Only Aliases** (Long-form modifiers)
Many "long-form" aliases have **no explicit field**—they're indicated only in the description text:

```json
{
  "name": "-size-sm",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "sm",
  "description": "Small."
}
{
  "name": "-size-small",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "small",
  "description": "Small. Long-form alias of `-size-sm`."
}
```

Both `-size-sm` and `-size-small` have different `value` properties (`"sm"` vs `"small"`) but apply the same semantic modifier. Since they lack `deprecated` or `alias` fields, the current [`isCanonicalConcreteModifier()` function](renderers/tinymce/src/cssdoc/model.ts#L49) treats **both as canonical**.

## Example: Button Component Modifiers

From [formats/components/dist/model.json](formats/components/dist/model.json#L100-L140), the button's size modifiers:

```json
{
  "name": "-size-lg",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "lg",
  "description": "Large."
},
{
  "name": "-size-large",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "large",
  "description": "Large. Long-form alias of `-size-lg`."
},
{
  "name": "-size-md",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "md",
  "description": "(default) medium size."
},
{
  "name": "-size-medium",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "medium",
  "description": "(default) medium size. Long-form alias of `-size-md`."
},
{
  "name": "-size-sm",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "sm",
  "description": "Small."
},
{
  "name": "-size-small",
  "prop": "size",
  "value": "small",
  "description": "Small. Long-form alias of `-size-sm`."
}
```

## Recommended Deduping Approach for TinyMCE

To dedupe canonical vs. alias in [renderers/tinymce/src/cssdoc/model.ts](renderers/tinymce/src/cssdoc/model.ts), consider:

1. **Filter on description text pattern** — exclude modifiers whose description matches `"Long-form alias of"`:
   ```typescript
   function isCanonicalConcreteModifier(modifier: CssModifier): boolean {
     const hasExplicitAlias = modifier.pattern || modifier.deprecated || modifier.alias || modifier.interaction;
     const isDescriptionAlias = modifier.description?.includes("Long-form alias of");
     return !hasExplicitAlias && !isDescriptionAlias;
   }
   ```

2. **Track the canonical form by prop+value** — when rendering suggestions, group aliases with their short forms:
   ```typescript
   const canonicalByPropValue = new Map<string, string>();
   for (const mod of modifiers) {
     const key = `${mod.prop}:${mod.value || ''}`;
     if (isCanonicalConcreteModifier(mod)) {
       canonicalByPropValue.set(key, mod.name);
     }
   }
   ```

3. **Consider adding an explicit `alias` field to long-form modifiers** during generation (in the components cssdoc authoring layer) for consistency, so future tooling doesn't need to parse descriptions.

